            <ArticleTitle>Does quality of life differ among headache diagnoses? Analysis using the medical outcomes study instrument.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To analyze the differences in quality of life associated with headache diagnoses using the Medical Outcomes Study Short Form Health Survey (SF-20).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A patient interview survey using the SF-20 Short Form Health Survey was conducted in a headache clinic within a multi-specialty group practice. All six health components of the SF-20 were included in the study. Headache diagnoses were made using IHS criteria.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">208 consecutive headache patients were studied. Patients with cluster headache had a significantly higher (worse) pain score (P &lt; 0.018) and higher percentage of patients with poor health due to pain (P &lt; 0.005) than patients with migraine headache. There were fewer cluster patients with poor health associated with physical functioning than tension-type (P &lt; 0.020) or mixed headache (P &lt; 0.022) patients. Poor health associated with social functioning was greater for cluster (P &lt; 0.011) and tension-type headache (P &lt; 0.015) than for migraine. There was a significantly higher percentage of tension-type headache patients with poor health associated with mental health (P &lt; 0.002) than patients with migraine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The SF-20 is a reliable and valid measure of quality of life for patients with different headache diagnoses. Distinct headache diagnoses are marked by unique patterns of impairment and quality of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
